Replace those with the repositories actually mounted here. A repository's own
`CLAUDE.md` is the authority on it; this file only records what is mounted.

## Working Across Repositories

- **The project directory is the only content root.** Each repository is reached
  through its junction and nowhere else. A repository added as a content root as
  well would be indexed twice, and every class would resolve to two files.
- **Each repository is a separate git root.** A change spanning two of them is two
  commits in two repositories, never one. `/ship` operates on one repository —
  tell it which, or run it in that repository.
- **Cross-repository calls are the reason for mounting them together.** One index
  means a consumer's call into a data layer resolves. It also means an edit in one
  repository can break another without either being open — check the consumers
  before changing a shared service signature.

## Shared Standards

The coding standards, structure references and agent instructions are the same for
every repository here. They are linked into each repository rather than duplicated,
so a repository opened on its own carries them too.

